  "notes": [
    "Regular -ER verb, reflexive — follows standard endings in every tense, no exceptions.",
    "Reflexive intensifier form of *comer*: *comerse algo* emphasizes finishing/consuming something completely, often used with food (*cómete la sopa* — \"eat up your soup\") rather than a true reflexive action.",
    "Past participle is regular: **comido**.",
    "Imperativo afirmativo reflexive pronouns attach to the end with a written accent to preserve stress (cómete, cómase, comámonos, cómanse); imperativo negativo places them separately in front (no te comas...).",
    "*Se comió todo el pastel él solo.* (\"He ate the whole cake by himself.\")",
    "*Cómete las verduras antes del postre.* (\"Eat up your vegetables before dessert.\")",
    "Related: *comer* (non-reflexive, \"to eat\"), *la comida* (food/meal), *comerse las uñas* (idiomatic, \"to bite one's nails\")."
  ],
